    "q-185468": {
      "dom_id": "step-q185468",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Suppose $p$ is directly proportional to $q.$ Given that $p=-35$ when $q=-7$, find the value of $p$ when $q=-6.$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Since $p$ varies directly with $q,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\np=kq.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $p=-35$ when $q=-7.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-35 & =k\\cdot (-7) \\\\ \\frac{(-35)}{(-7)} & =k \\\\ \\frac{35}{7} & =k \\\\ 5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\np=5q.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $q=-6$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =5\\cdot (-6)=-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
